>> kube 11 : Jobs and cronjob in kubernetes cluster 
***********************************************************************************************

Jobs : Job will run (depending upon its configuration) and exits. It's not like pod/replicas deployment,
       Jobs are created to do some specific tasks. Jobs doesn't runs for forever, it just runs for completion. 

Terms used related to Job. 
1. Default run : It will simple run and completed the job with specific task mentioned in job description. 
2. Killing pod and restart pod: In this case ,if we kill the pod before job get completed (exits with successful rc code i.e 1), it will keep on spin the new pod until job get completed. 
3. completions: It will sequential complete the job , for eg, completions value is 2 than job will run 2 times one after the other. 
4. parallelism: It tell how many job can run in parallel , for eg if completions value is set to 10 and parallelism value is 2 then it will iterate 5 times with running two job at same time. 
5. backofflimit: It specific how many times job should try to spin pod if job keeps on falling to complete itself. Default value of backofflimit is 6. 
6. activeDeadlineSeconds: It is used when you don't want your job to keep of running after specific time period, for eg, if your job take on an average of 20 seconds for completion and we don't want your job to exceeds 20 seconds to complete it's execution then activedeadlineSeconds came in picture . 

Cronjob: It is used to schedule a job/task as per your requirement.
# Note : By default kuberenets keep the records of last 3 succesfully completed cronjob and one last failed job for debugging purpose .  
#  Note : When we delete the cronjob , it may or may not delete all the pod , if any pods left behind we have clean it manually. 

Terms used related to cronjob: 
1. successfulJobsHistroyLimit: it hold the records of successfully completed job, by default its 3, but we can alter as per ours liking. 
2. failedJobsHistoryLimit: It hold the records of failed job, by default its  1 , but we can alter as per our requirement. 
3. suspend : It will stop launching/schduling new cronjobs,but keep in mind already running cronjobs will not be effected by it. 
# Note : Imperative approach to change suspend status
kubectl patch cronjob cronjobname -p '{"spec":{"suspend":false}}'
4. concurrencyPolicy: Running multiple cronjob at the same time. 
   concurrencyPolicy can have three possible value : allow(default value), replace and forbid. 
   a) allow : this is default concurrency policy means it allow multiple job to run at same time. 
   b) forbid: this will not allow second job to start if there is already job running at that time. 
   c) replace : this will replace the already running job.  

