>> kube 12 : Init Containers in kubernetes 
***********************************************************************************************

## Init containers : 
>> Link:  https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/

Init Containers are special container that run for completion before the actual container. 
It can contains utilities or setup scripts not present in container image. 

## Init containers are exactly like regular containers, except:

1. Init containers always run to completion.
2. Each init container must complete successfully before the next one starts.(runs in sequential manner)

If init container fails (for whatssoever reason), the kubelet repeatedly restart the init container until its succeeds.
To avoid multiple restart of init container we can set restartPolicy to never and if init container fails ,then kubernets treats overall pod as failure.    

# Note : init containers do not support lifecycle, livenessProbe, readinessProbe, or startupProbe because they must run to completion before the Pod can be ready.

## Advantages of Init container 
1. Init containers can contain utilities or custom code for setup that are not present in an app image.
2. The application image builder and deployer roles can work independently without the need to jointly build a single app image.
3. Init containers can run with a different view of the filesystem than app containers in the same Pod. Consequently, they can be given access to Secrets that app containers cannot access.
4. Init container can be used as prerequisite , as it run for completion otherwise kuberenets will let app container to start.
5. By using init we can keep unnecessary tool separate and limit the attack surface of your app container image. 

