>> kube 13 :  Using Persistent Volumes and Claims in Kubernetes Cluster
***********************************************************************************************
## Why do we need persistent volume?
Lifecycle of pod is very inconsistent, and data stored in container in the pod will be lost once pod gets killed. 
In order to preserve the data of container in pod we use persistent volume. 
Once the persistent volume is provisioned then we can use it via pvc (persistent volume claim)
For eg: let assume we have pv of 10GB , and we need pv of 1GB ,then 1GB (out of 10GB) of pv will be allocated to us . And it can't be used/claimed by anyone else unless we using it. 


## Persistent Volume ane claims steps: 
1. create a persistent volume.
2. create a persistent volume claim 
3. Pod that uses the pvc ---> pv 


## ReclaimPolicy of PV
1. Retain ---> Default --> once pod is delete your data will remain intact and no other pvc can claim . 
2. Recycle --> used for dynamic provisioning (not used  anyone more )
3. Delete  --> Once we delete pod and pvc then it will delete pv data as well (only if directory is under /tmp directory). 

## Access mode of PV:
1. RWO (Read write once) --> only first node pods can read and write and rest other nodes can only read. 
2. RWM (Read write many) --> multiple node pods can read and write. 
3. RO (Read only) --> only able to read across multiple nodes. 

Note : Hostpath is not used in production as filesystem is mounted inside a particular node(volume is specfic to node).  

