Kubernetes is Deprecating Docker?!
***********************************************************************************************
## Kubernetes is Deprecating Docker?
>> Kubernetes and Docker seems like strong team mates, one has expertise in converting your end application into containers and other  has expertise in managing/orchestrate those containers. 
>> If we see from the outside it really doesn't make any sense why Kubernetes is deprecating docker support. 
>> To understand why this happened , we need to understand the working of both of these tools under the hood.

## Why is Kubernetes Deprecating Docker?
>> Kubernetes does not actually handle the process of running containers on a machine. Instead, it relies on another piece of software called a container runtime.
>> The container runtime runs containers on a host, and Kubernetes tells the container runtime on each host what to do.
>> Kubernetes works with all container runtimes that implement a standard known as the Container Runtime Interface (CRI).
>> Docker does not implement the Container Runtime Interface (CRI). Kubernetes implemented the Docker shim, an additional layer to serve as an interface between Kubernetes and Docker. 
>> When Kubernetes was launched in the market, there was no major player other than docker and at that time its make sense that you are spending our resources to make docker compatible with Kubernetes. 
>> Now, however, there are plenty of runtimes available that implement the CRI, and it no longer makes sense for Kubernetes to maintain special support for Docker.
>> Docker is not actually a container runtime! It’s actually a collection of tools that sits on top of a container runtime called containerd.
>> So its make sense that Kubernetes can easily bypass Docker(middle man) and use containerd as container runtime . 
>>  containerd or CRI-O emerges as the major alternatives which will replace docker in Container runtime interface..
